---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import ColorConvertTool from '../../components/tools/ColorConvertTool.astro';
import { toolBySlug } from '../../data/tools';
import { parseColor, rgbToHex, formatRgb, formatHsl, contrastRatio, SAMPLE_COLOR } from '../../lib/color-convert';

const tool = toolBySlug('color-converter');

// Worked example and every value below come from the real engine at build time.
const sample = (parseColor(SAMPLE_COLOR) as { ok: true; value: { r: number; g: number; b: number; a: number } }).value;
const sampleHex = rgbToHex(sample);
const sampleRgb = formatRgb(sample);
const sampleHsl = formatHsl(sample);
const onBlack = contrastRatio(sample, { r: 0, g: 0, b: 0, a: 1 });
const onWhite = contrastRatio(sample, { r: 255, g: 255, b: 255, a: 1 });

const roundTrip = (parseColor(formatHsl((parseColor('rgb(30, 144, 255)') as { ok: true; value: { r: number; g: number; b: number; a: number } }).value)) as { ok: true; value: { r: number; g: number; b: number } }).value;

const err = (fn: () => { ok: true } | { ok: false; error: string }) => {
  const r = fn();
  return r.ok ? '' : r.error.replace(/\.$/, '');
};
const badHexErr = err(() => parseColor('#zzz'));
const shortRgbErr = err(() => parseColor('rgb(1,2)'));

const steps = [
  'Paste a color as a hex code, <code>rgb()</code>/<code>rgba()</code>, or <code>hsl()</code>/<code>hsla()</code>, or pick one with the swatch next to the box. The format is auto-detected.',
  'The preview shows the color with black and with white sample text on it; each carries its contrast ratio, labelled AA, AAA or a fail, and the better of the two is marked <strong>best</strong>.',
  'Read the HEX, RGB and HSL values under the preview, all computed from the same parsed color, and the <strong>Darker and lighter</strong> strip: the same hue in 10-point lightness steps.',
  'Click any value or any swatch in the strip to copy it; <strong>Load sample</strong> inserts a worked example.',
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'What do the AA and AAA labels mean?',
    a: 'They are the WCAG 2 contrast thresholds for normal-size text against a background: 4.5:1 or higher passes AA, 7:1 or higher passes AAA, and anything below 4.5:1 fails both. Large text (about 18pt or 14pt bold and up) has lower thresholds, 3:1 and 4.5:1, which this page does not compute separately; a background that fails for normal text may still be fine for a large heading.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does converting to HSL and back change the numbers slightly?',
    a: `HSL rounds hue to a whole degree and saturation/lightness to a whole percent, so a small amount of rounding drift is normal: <code>rgb(30, 144, 255)</code> converted to HSL and back becomes <code>rgb(${roundTrip.r}, ${roundTrip.g}, ${roundTrip.b})</code> (each channel off by at most 1). This is a property of HSL itself, not a bug in the conversion.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Does the # matter in a hex code?',
    a: 'No. It is optional. Both "#3366ff" and "3366ff" parse the same way, so you can paste a hex value straight out of a design tool’s color picker without editing it first.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does the “best” label on the preview mean?',
    a: 'It marks whichever of pure black or pure white text has the higher contrast ratio against the color you entered, a quick answer to "should this button’s label be black or white text" without comparing two numbers yourself.',
  },
];
---

<ToolLayout {tool} {steps} {faq}>
  <ColorConvertTool />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>One color, every format design tools expect</h2>
    <p>
      Design tools, CSS, and image editors each tend to default to a different color notation: a design file exports hex,
      a CSS-in-JS library wants an <code>rgba()</code> string, a design system's tokens are defined in HSL because it is
      easier for a human to reason about ("a bit darker" is just a lower lightness number). Converting by hand between
      them means re-deriving the math each time; this tool parses whichever one you paste and shows all three at once,
      plus whether it is safe to put text on top of.
    </p>

    <h2>Example</h2>
    <p>The color <code>{SAMPLE_COLOR}</code> is, in every format:</p>
    <pre><code>{sampleHex}
{sampleRgb}
{sampleHsl}</code></pre>
    <p>and has a contrast ratio of <strong>{onBlack}</strong> against black text and <strong>{onWhite}</strong> against white text.</p>

    <h2>Options explained</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>HEX / RGB / HSL</strong> are computed together from one parsed color; there is no separate "convert to"
        step, so pasting any one of the three immediately shows the other two.</li>
      <li><strong>The preview's contrast badges</strong> (on the "Black text" and "White text" samples) apply the WCAG 2
        formula to the color as a background, assuming plain black or white text on top. See the FAQ for what the AA/AAA
        labels and the <strong>best</strong> mark mean.</li>
      <li><strong>Load sample</strong> inserts <code>{SAMPLE_COLOR}</code>; <strong>Clear</strong> empties the box.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Edge cases and common errors</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Invalid hex digits.</strong> <code>#zzz</code> stops with "{badHexErr}": every character after the
        optional <code>#</code> must be 0-9 or a-f.</li>
      <li><strong>Wrong number of values.</strong> <code>rgb(1,2)</code> stops with "{shortRgbErr}": <code>rgb()</code>
        needs exactly three values (red, green, blue) and an optional fourth (alpha); <code>hsl()</code> needs hue,
        saturation and lightness the same way.</li>
      <li><strong>A bare hex-looking word is accepted as a color.</strong> Because the leading <code>#</code> is optional,
        a 3, 4, 6 or 8-letter word made only of the letters a-f and digits (like <code>cafe</code>, <code>dead</code> or <code>beef</code>) parses as a color rather than being rejected as text. This is intentional (it lets you paste a
        hex value straight from a tool that omits the <code>#</code>), but it means this box is not a safe way to detect
        "is this string definitely a color".</li>
      <li><strong>HSL round-trip rounding</strong>: see the FAQ.</li>
      <li><strong>Percentage and slash syntax both work</strong> in <code>rgb()</code>: <code>rgb(100%, 0%, 0%)</code> and <code>rgb(255 0 0 / 50%)</code> (the modern space-and-slash form, alongside the older comma form) both parse.</li>
      <li><strong>Alpha only shows where it matters.</strong> A fully opaque color's hex is 6 digits and its RGB/HSL have
        no fourth value; add any alpha below 1 (via <code>rgba()</code>, <code>hsla()</code>, or an 8-digit hex) and all
        three outputs grow an alpha component to match.</li>
      <li><strong>Contrast of a translucent color is shown as if it were solid</strong>, and the badges say
        "(as solid)". A contrast ratio needs one opaque color, and a translucent color only becomes one once it
        is laid over something: <code>rgba(255, 0, 0, 0.3)</code> over white is a pale pink, over black a dark maroon.
        Check the final blended color instead if the background is known. The color picker also has no alpha, so
        picking a color always gives an opaque one.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Do the same thing in code</h2>
    <pre><code>// CSS itself understands all of these natively: no conversion needed at all
// if the value only needs to reach a browser:
.button {'{'} background: {SAMPLE_COLOR}; {'}'}
.button {'{'} background: rgb(51, 102, 255); {'}'}
.button {'{'} background: hsl(225, 100%, 60%); {'}'}</code></pre>
    <p class="muted">Conversion only matters once something other than CSS needs the value in a specific format: a
      design token file, an image-processing library, or a contrast check like the one this page runs.</p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
